## The CPU-queue counters, and their limit on this bench
`show platform counter sdma` exposes Marvell SDMA receive-queue counters.
  rxDmaPcktCnt[0] -- a plain cumulative counter. copy-to-cpu lands here, and it is CLEAN:
                     it sits at 0 on this bench and moves only when a copy-to-cpu rule matches.
  rxDmaPcktCnt[6] -- CLEAR-ON-READ, and it carries the background control traffic this bench
                     generates continuously (RSTP BPDUs, LLDP, and loop-protection detect
                     frames at one per second on every port). Measured idle rate: 40 counts in
                     8 s. Ten test packets against that floor is not separable, so queue 6 is
                     NOT used as evidence in these cases. Recorded so nobody reads its absence
                     as a negative result.

## Steps and evidence

### STEP 0 -- STP baseline before any CPU-action ACL
x960-56YSQ#show spanning-tree brief
  Default: Root Id 8000:0000cd423029
  Default: Bridge Id 8000:e01aead472cb
  sa1             8000:0000cd423029    8195       Rootport         Forwarding
  port1.0.10      8000:e01aead472cb    8392       Designated       Forwarding
  port1.0.48      8000:e01aead472cb    83b8       Designated       Forwarding
  Captured because the guide warns that CPU-bound ACL actions can disturb control traffic, and
  T44989's precondition cites CR30477 for STP interference. RSTP is running on all three units.

### STEP 1 -- apply copy-to-cpu and prove BOTH halves of its definition
The ACL guide defines copy-to-cpu as "copy to the CPU AND forward". Proving only the CPU half
would not distinguish it from send-to-cpu; proving only the forwarding half would not
distinguish it from no rule at all. Both are required.
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list hardware t44991
x960-56YSQ(config-ip-hw-acl)#copy-to-cpu ip 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.48
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#access-group t44991
x960-56YSQ#show access-list
  Hardware IP access list t44991
      4 copy-to-cpu ip 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32

  HALF ONE -- copied to the CPU:
x960-56YSQ#show platform counter sdma        (before, then after 8 pings)
              0 : sdmaRegs.rxDmaPcktCnt[0]   ->      8 : sdmaRegs.rxDmaPcktCnt[0]
x960-56YSQ#show access-list counters t44991
      Packet Hits: 8
  CPU receive-queue 0 advanced from 0 to 8, EXACTLY matching the 8 ACL hits.

  HALF TWO -- still forwarded:
host# ping -c 8 -i 0.3 -I 10.38.22.65 10.38.22.70
  0% packet loss                            <-- the far side still received and replied
  => STEP 1 PASS: matching frames were copied to the CPU and were ALSO still forwarded to the
     far side. The exact agreement between the CPU queue delta and the ACL hit count is what
     makes the CPU half positive proof rather than coincidence, and the 0% loss is what
     distinguishes copy-to-cpu from send-to-cpu.

### STEP 2 -- STP undisturbed (CR30477 watch)
x960-56YSQ#show spanning-tree brief
  Default: Root Id 8000:0000cd423029        <-- unchanged
  sa1             8000:0000cd423029    8195       Rootport         Forwarding
  port1.0.48      8000:e01aead472cb    83b8       Designated       Forwarding
  => STEP 2 PASS: the root bridge, the root port and every forwarding port are unchanged
     across the copy-to-cpu step. No STP disturbance.

### STEP 3 -- the contrast that gives this case its meaning
Run back to back with T44989 on the identical setup, binding point and traffic:

  action        ACL hits   CPU queue 0    forwarded to the far side?
  copy-to-cpu       8        0 -> 8       YES  (0% packet loss)
  send-to-cpu       8        0 -> 0       NO   (100% packet loss)

  Both rules matched the same 8 packets, so the difference is entirely in what the switch did
  with them. copy-to-cpu also uses a different CPU path: queue 0 moves for copy-to-cpu and
  does not move at all for send-to-cpu.
  => STEP 3 PASS: the pair behaves exactly to the guide's definitions and each is the other's
     control.

VERDICT: PASS -- a named hardware ACL with the copy-to-cpu action, bound to a port, copies
matching frames to CPU receive-queue 0 (0 -> 8, exactly matching its 8 ACL hits) AND still
forwards them to the far side (0% packet loss). Spanning tree was unchanged across the step.
Run back to back with send-to-cpu on identical setup, the two differ precisely as documented:
same hit count, opposite forwarding outcome, different CPU queue.
